S2EN←('Not long ago, things looked grim here. Everything was buried under sand, and the danger was far from small.' 'Behold, reader: this green land, which you see stretching all the way to the sea, was until recently a sea of sand.') ('Mountains of sand rose up and swept forward whenever the wind came, driving many a person from house and home.' 'Wherever the wind turned, it sent waves of sand flying like mountains, which swallowed field and forest. Streams, fields, meadows, villages and cottages were seen buried entirely in the sand. Each field once overrun at once threatened the next with the same violence of sand.') ('That is why King Frederik, for the good and benefit of the country, had the work taken in hand with wagons, men and horses, and had the loose ground sown as far as one could reach, so that grass and straw now grow where nothing grew before.' 'Until King Frederik the Fourth was the first to relieve the distress, gave the sand firm boundaries and here and there adorned it with a green covering.') ('Our Christian the Sixth, our glory in the North – through him the whole work has been brought to completion.' 'Whereupon his great son, Christian the Sixth, added what was still lacking, until he completed the fine work, so that the sand which would have ravaged Zealand must now, as fertile ground, feed many animals.') ('May his line and offspring endure as long as dust and sand can be found around the shores of the sea.' 'Honour, then, the wise counsel of the fathers of the land, which God’s power has blessed, and wish that their line on earth may become like the sand by the sea, and everlasting.')

